   "whyItIsNeeded": "native-evidence section 10 labels its second column `Cause (nativeCause)` and then fills three of its rows with values that are not NativeCause members: `the unresolved edge classes; partial/not-attempted stage` for `resolution-incomplete`, `exports-open, exports-unknown` for `external-consumers-unknown`, and `compiler-inferred present` for `derivation-policy-unmet`. Each refuses schema validation as a nativeCause (`'computed-member-access' is not valid under any of the given schemas`), so the only representable value was null - which the NativeCause description itself defines as `a disclosure was owed and not made`. Worse, nothing compared the pair to anything: on the frozen reference a `resolution-incomplete` entry carrying the unrelated but schema-valid `lockfile-missing`, or relabelled `capability-missing`, or a `derivation-policy-unmet` with an empty derivationKinds, or an `external-consumers-unknown` over `exportsClosed: closed`, each closed a Run. A cause vocabulary that nothing derives and nothing compares leaves the disclosure optional in practice - the same argument that added the three body-language-* members, carried through to the rest of the table.",
   "whyNotMoreEnumMembers": "Adding NativeCause members was rejected on the merits, not for compatibility. `resolution-incomplete` has a genuinely SET-VALUED cause: an entry can exhibit up to all sixteen UnresolvedEdgeKindV1 classes at once, and a scalar cannot carry them, so enum members would either lose information or force an arbitrary pick - the multi-cause defect the finding names. All three rows already have closed, structured, independently DERIVED carriers in the same record: unresolvedEdgeClasses (which RC-2 already forces to equal the admitted unresolved-edge facts), ClosedWorldV2.exportsClosed, and derivationKinds. The correction is to NAME those carriers and ENFORCE the join, not to duplicate them in a scalar.",
   "nullIsNotAnEscape": "Where a row says nativeCause MUST be null, that null no longer records an unmade disclosure: the row names the field that carries the cause instead, and admission REFUSES unless that field actually carries it. A `resolution-incomplete` entry whose resolutionCompleteness claims `complete` is refused, so `null` cannot be used to say nothing.",
-  "multiCauseAndPrecedence": "An entry carries ONE deficiency and simultaneously carries EVERY carrier. The carriers are structurally independent - unresolvedEdgeClasses is an array holding all applicable classes at once, and closedWorld and derivationKinds are separate fields - so a scalar deficiency loses nothing: the other simultaneous conditions remain fully disclosed in their own fields. Which single member is declared when several apply is the section 10 precedence order, applied by the owning sufficiency evaluation (PRECEDENCE_V2, most specific first) against a REQUIREMENT. That selection is deliberately NOT re-derived from the entry alone, and the limit is stated rather than worked around: several conditions are requirement-relative (external-consumer state matters only for a universal negative about an exported target; the confidence floor lives in the Requirement, not the entry), and RC-3 makes `coverage: complete` with `state: incomplete` and NO deficiency a valid honest entry. Forcing a deficiency from exhibited entry evidence would therefore refuse lawful Runs. What IS decidable from the entry is the direction enforced here: a DECLARED deficiency must be SUPPORTED by the entry's own committed evidence.",
+  "multiCauseAndPrecedence": "An entry carries ONE deficiency and simultaneously carries every carrier this registry names. What each carrier RETAINS differs, and an earlier revision wrongly generalised the strongest case to all of them by saying the scalar `loses nothing`. The accurate statement is per carrier: resolutionCompleteness.unresolvedEdgeClasses is a genuine SET and retains EVERY applicable unresolved class at once, already forced by RC-2 to equal the admitted unresolved-edge facts of the view; closedWorld and derivationKinds are independent fields that retain their own state whatever the deficiency is; but input-closure-incomplete's carrier is a SINGLE SCALAR NativeCause, and a Run can genuinely be missing several inputs at once - two absent crates, or an absent crate AND an unavailable generated file. For that row the retained cause is a SELECTED one, not the full set. See selectedScalarCauseLimitation. Which single deficiency is declared when several apply is the section 10 precedence order, applied by the owning sufficiency evaluation against a REQUIREMENT. That selection is deliberately NOT re-derived from the entry alone, and the limit is stated rather than worked around: several conditions are requirement-relative (external-consumer state matters only for a universal negative about an exported target; the confidence floor lives in the Requirement, not the entry), and RC-3 makes `coverage: complete` with `state: incomplete` and NO deficiency a valid honest entry. Forcing a deficiency from exhibited entry evidence would therefore refuse lawful Runs. What IS decidable from the entry is the direction enforced here: a DECLARED deficiency must be SUPPORTED by the entry's own committed evidence.",
   "retainedVersusPublicRoute": "Two different routes and neither substitutes for the other. RETAINED: the whole ViewEntryV3 lives inside the coverage2 record, whose payload digest and schema digest the Run commits, so every carrier named below is retained evidence a verifier can re-derive. PUBLIC: the DEFICIENCY is projected outward as its own closed DomainDetailCode - `resolution-incomplete`, `external-consumers-unknown` and `derivation-policy-unmet` are all already members - and the CAUSE travels as typed detail in the coverage2 record the host termination names by its coverageId, exactly as section 10 has always said for the nativeCause-carried rows. No new public code is added and none is needed.",
