+ "x-opensip-public-route-registry": {
+  "standing": "Normative and CLOSED. The public route of every internal decision key the capability vocabulary and Coverage cause guards introduce. Nothing here is a new public DomainDetailCode, D9 class, code or exit: each key normalizes at the host boundary to a code ALREADY in the closed public detail registry, which is what that registry's own aliasRule requires (`internal decision keys are normalized at the host boundary and cannot appear in public DomainDetailCode`). Existing precedents for the same mechanism are native.explicit-root-grammar -> PROJECT.EXPLICIT_PATH_INVALID, native.too-many-units -> PROJECT.WORKSPACE_UNIT_LIMIT and provider-unavailable/capability-missing -> native.capability-unavailable.",
+  "phaseAndAuthorityLaw": "The distinction is the EXISTING one. An invalid REQUEST is the caller's analysis-spec being wrong: malformed vocabulary is CONFIG.INVALID, which admission section 1.1 already covers by forbidding configuration that `selects an unregistered capability`, and a well-formed request for a cell that carries no promise is REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE. An invalid authenticated RELEASE DECLARATION is the HOST'S OWN authenticated input being wrong, before any Plan exists and with no worker spawned - the phase the native context row already occupies - so it is REQUEST.PRECONDITION_FAILED. A user cannot cause the second and a release cannot cause the first.",
+  "unsupportedTypedIsNotOnThisTable": "An UNSUPPORTED-TYPED request is admitted and ANSWERED, never refused: the Run closes and its Coverage carries `unknown` with the cell's own deficiency and cause. A compiler-free references request under syntax-only closes with unknown / language-tier-unsupported / capability-missing; a capability the release has not declared available closes with unknown / provider-unavailable / capability-missing. Neither may be converted into an admission refusal.",
