+  "recordsEditOwnedByRoot": "The aliasRule also says an UNKNOWN internal key is refused, so a host normalizer needs these pairs present in public-detail-registry.v1.json#/internalAliases. That file is a RECORDS artifact owned by root integration, not by this contract, and it is deliberately NOT edited here. The exact pairs are published above so the records edit is mechanical: each `keys` entry maps to its `publicDetail`, and every target is already a member of that registry.",
+  "enforcedAt": "native_evidence_model admit_requested_capabilities / admit_release_capability_registry / deficiency_cause_faults, and foundation close_run, which re-runs the requested-capability admission over the retained analysis-spec (ANALYSIS_SPEC_CAPABILITY:) and the producer Coverage admission over the retained payload.",
+  "originatingBoundaryLaw": "admission-and-qualification section 1 already fixes the actor/layer split: `Invalid external input is an admission rejection with the existing carrier's D9 code; an invalid host-generated internal layer is a host invariant fault.` The SAME internal key therefore takes a different public code depending on WHERE the bad value entered, and a blanket request-rejected for every capability fault would contradict that existing law. Three originating boundaries are distinguished, and only they: (a) EXTERNAL configuration - a capability the user configured through Config2/CLI analysis.capabilities - is invalid external input on that carrier, so it takes that carrier's code, CONFIG.INVALID; (b) HOST-GENERATED analysis-spec - a retained or directly supplied spec the host itself produced, refused at the analysis-spec boundary or at Run closure - is an invalid host-generated internal layer and is a HOST INVARIANT FAULT, not a user configuration error, and must not be reported as CONFIG.INVALID; (c) HOST-SUPPLIED AUTHENTICATED RELEASE REGISTRY - a malformed declaration the host supplies before any Plan - is likewise host-supplied and takes the precondition route the native context row already occupies, REQUEST.PRECONDITION_FAILED. The distinction between (b) and (c) is which host input is wrong, and both are separated from (a) by actor.",
+  "helperDoesNotGuessOrigin": "The pure admission helpers - admit_requested_capabilities, admit_release_capability_registry - are passed ROWS and nothing about provenance, so they emit the internal decision key and NOTHING ELSE. Selecting the public code from the originating boundary is the HOST's normalization step, using the event context it already holds, exactly as the aliasRule describes. A helper inventing an origin it was not passed would be a guess, and this table is written so it never has to."
  },
